\section{First section}

Lets analyze the \texttt{iris} data:

<<echo=FALSE>>=
lmfit <- lm(Sepal.Length ~ Species, data = iris)
summary(lmfit)
@


\subsection{Results in a table}
We want to show the results in a table. We can print R data frames (or matrices) as tables. As an example, see the following Table \ref{coeftable}.

<<results='asis', echo=FALSE>>=
library(xtable)
sumy <- summary(lmfit)
coeftbl <- data.frame(apply(sumy$coefficients, 2, round, digits=2), 
                      check.names = FALSE)
### print table:
print(xtable(coeftbl, caption = 'Results of my linear model', label = 'coeftable'), 
      include.rownames=FALSE, table.placement="H")
#' One needs to add sanitize.text.function = function(x){x} in the print call if 
#' there are latex commands that should be executed inside the table.
@

\subsection{Adding a figure}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
<<echo=FALSE, fig.dim = c(9, 6)>>=
### R plot:
boxplot(Sepal.Length ~ Species, data = iris)
@
\caption{This is an iris data boxplot.}
\label{boxplot1}
\end{figure}

As can be seen in Figure \ref{boxplot1}, there is a clear difference between species.


\section{Adding a figure from a file}
<< echo=FALSE, results='hide'>>=
### Create figure file:
jpeg(filename = './boxplot.jpeg', width = 10, height = 10, units = 'cm', 
     pointsize = 10, res = 300)
boxplot(Sepal.Length ~ Species, data = iris, col='blue')
dev.off()
@

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\setlength{\fboxrule}{0cm}\fbox{\includegraphics[width=0.75\columnwidth]{boxplot.jpeg}}
\caption{This is again a boxplot.}
\label{boxplotblue}
\end{figure}


Figure \ref{boxplotblue} shows a blue boxplot.


